      <section>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Is the Steelcase Leap Plus good for very tall people?</h3>
        <p>Yes, but only if you order it correctly. The Leap Plus remains the strongest ergonomic chair for users 6'3" and above: the seat depth adjusts up to 19.75", the back height reaches 25.5", and the 500 lb capacity covers larger builds. On seat height, the standard cylinder stops at 19.5" — lower than the Gesture (21") and the Aeron Size C (20.5"). The optional 5" cylinder (about $63) shifts the range to 17.5"–22.5", the highest ceiling of the three. Users 6'4" and above must specify it at order time. Seat height figures per the <a href="https://www.steelcase.com/content/uploads/2020/10/Seating-Leap-Seat.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Steelcase Seating Specification Guide</a>.</p>

        <h3>How does the Leap Plus compare to the standard Steelcase Leap?</h3>
        <p>The Leap Plus is larger than the standard Leap in most dimensions, but not in seat height. Seat width widens to 22" (from 19.5"), back height increases to 25.5" (from 22"), seat depth extends to 19.75" (from 18.5"), and weight capacity rises to 500 lbs (from 400). Seat height goes the other way: the standard Leap adjusts 15.5"–20.5", while the standard Leap Plus adjusts 15.5"–19.5" — an inch lower at maximum. Both offer an optional taller cylinder (17"–24" on the Leap, 17.5"–22.5" on the Leap Plus). Both use LiveBack and the Natural Glide System. The Plus trades seat-height range for width, back height, and capacity.</p>

        <h3>Does the Steelcase Leap Plus have a break-in period?</h3>
        <p>Yes, but it's modest — approximately 2–3 weeks. The foam seat starts slightly firm and softens with use. This is less pronounced than the Gesture's 3–4 week break-in. Users switching from mesh chairs should expect a different feel overall, as foam retains more heat and provides different pressure distribution.</p>

        <h3>Is the Leap Plus worth the price for tall users?</h3>
        <p>For users 6'3" and above, yes. The seat depth and back height advantages over the Gesture are concrete, not marginal. On seat height it only beats the Gesture with the optional 5" cylinder — that configuration reaches 22.5" against the Gesture's 21", while the standard cylinder stops at 19.5" and loses to it. Refurbished units at 40–60% of new price are widely available and perform identically once remanufactured, but confirm which cylinder a used unit carries before buying at tall heights. For daily 8-hour use, a correctly specified Leap Plus is the most practical premium investment.</p>

        <h3>What height range is the Steelcase Leap Plus designed for?</h3>
        <p>The Leap Plus accommodates users from approximately 5'8" to 6'7"+, but the usable range depends on the cylinder. With the standard cylinder (15.5"–19.5") it suits users up to roughly 6'2". With the optional 5" cylinder (17.5"–22.5") it covers users through 6'6" and many at 6'7". Users above 6'6" should also verify the 25.5" back height provides adequate shoulder-blade coverage for their torso length.</p>
      </section>

